assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       A timer created with SIGEV_SIGNAL, SIGRTMIN (SA_SIGINFO set) and
           sigev_value 99 generates SIGRTMIN when it expires (accepted
           within 5 s), with si_code SI_TIMER and si_value 99.
uses       XSH.sigtimedwait.005
note       CLOCK_REALTIME is used: timer_create() support for
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC is unconditional only in Issue 8.
nomutant   the fault would be in timer_create(), which has no module
           (and so no shim) yet
test       t/001-sigev-signal.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       A timer created with SIGEV_THREAD (attributes NULL) calls
           sigev_notify_function with sigev_value (a pointer) when it
           expires (within 5 s), in a thread other than the main
           thread.
note       CLOCK_REALTIME is used: timer_create() support for
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC is unconditional only in Issue 8.
nomutant   the fault would be in timer_create(), which has no module
           (and so no shim) yet
test       t/002-sigev-thread.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       A timer created with SIGEV_NONE (sigev_signo SIGRTMIN) generates no
           signal when it expires: 400 ms after arming it for 100 ms,
           neither SIGRTMIN nor SIGALRM is pending (by sigpending() and by a
           zero-timeout sigtimedwait()).
uses       XSH.sigpending.001
note       CLOCK_REALTIME is used: timer_create() support for
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC is unconditional only in Issue 8.
nomutant   the fault would be in timer_create(), which has no module
           (and so no shim) yet
test       t/003-sigev-none.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGRTMIN blocked (SA_SIGINFO set), values 1 and 2 queued by
           sigqueue() and then a kill() of SIGRTMIN, every occurrence is
           accepted (sigtimedwait(), 5 s then none left): the SI_QUEUE ones
           are values 1 and 2 in that order; an SI_USER one may come
           anywhere.
uses       XSH.sigqueue.001 XSH.sigtimedwait.001
note       Whether the kill() occurrence is queued, and where, is not
           specified; only the occurrences sigqueue() made must stay in
           FIFO order (standard question Q-17).
test       t/004-kill-keeps-queue.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGRTMIN caught with SA_SIGINFO and two values queued while
           blocked, on unblocking the first handler call still sees
           SIGRTMIN pending (one more queued), and after the second call it
           is not pending.
uses       XSH.sigpending.001 XSH.sigqueue.001
note       sigpending() is async-signal-safe; the handler blocks
           SIGRTMIN while it runs, so the second occurrence waits
           pending.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/005-pending-while-queued.c
